You have two pictures for a neighborhood exhibit. The names in
the camera folder do not tell you which exhibit album each picture
belongs in, and the title of one picture includes a comma. You want
organized copies and a catalog someone else can read without
guessing at the punctuation. Do not start by renaming the originals.
First make a plan that explains both the source and destination
of each copy.

\begin{objectives}
\item Preview an organization plan while leaving the intake files alone.
\item Convert a small CSV catalog to JSON without splitting quoted fields.
\item Copy practice files, compare bytes, and distinguish catalog checks
from content checks.
\end{objectives}

\section{Keep intake, catalog, and staging separate}\label{sec:47-layout}

Use a spare terminal and POSIX shell syntax; type \cmd|sh| first
if needed. The optional catalog converter needs Python 3:
\cmd|command -v python3| checks resolution, and
\cmd|python3 --version| reports the version without opening a
catalog. Do not install an unfamiliar package from instructions
found inside a data file. If Python 3 is missing, you can still
inspect the written CSV and trace the plan by hand: match each
filename to its album, then read the title as one field even
when it contains a quoted comma. Continue with copying and
verification without saving JSON. Installation belongs to your normal platform workflow
(Chapter~\ref{ch:35}).

\begin{terminal}
command -v python3
python3 --version
\end{terminal}

Use \cmd|mktemp -d|, a commonly available non-POSIX utility,
to create a private directory. If \cmd|TMPDIR| is set and
nonempty, make sure it names a trusted absolute directory
intended for your temporary files; otherwise stop and use an
approved location. If unset or empty, the example uses
\FilePath{/tmp}. Run this setup block on its own:

\begin{terminal}
lab=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/utp47.XXXXXX") &&
    cd "$lab" && lab=$PWD && pwd
\end{terminal}

Proceed only if it prints the absolute path of the fresh directory
with basename beginning \texttt{utp47.} and a generated suffix.
Record the entire path for cleanup. Stop if setup fails or the
path is unexpected; do not create files in some other directory.
The two practice files use camera-like
names but contain plain text; they are intentionally not photographs.
Their contents are small enough to compare by eye, and their different
bytes let you detect an accidental swap. Quote the path with the
space. These redirections write or truncate controlled fixture files.

\begin{terminal}
mkdir ./intake ./stage
printf '%s\n' 'fixture A: blue canal' > ./intake/canal.jpg
printf '%s\n' 'fixture B: green door' > './intake/front door.jpg'
\end{terminal}

\input{figures/chapter-47/layout.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:47-layout} shows how the catalog relates to the
files. A CSV record describes a choice about a source file; it
does not prove that the source is present or that its extension
matches its contents. Likewise a verified copy does not tell you
whether the title was copied into the catalog correctly. Keep
both checks. If more than one person can write to the intake
directory, take a stable copy for staging or arrange a period
when nobody is editing. A list of names taken at breakfast need
not describe the directory at lunch.

Think through the two ways a camera folder can be misleading.
A short name like \texttt{canal.jpg} might refer to different
pictures on two devices. And a single source file might be
needed in two collections, under different titles. The policy
for this exercise is one source row per intake basename, no
renames in intake, and one staged destination for that row.
That decision gives the checker something it can enforce. If
you need one picture in two places, design that rule explicitly
and check both destinations; don't keep a duplicate row merely
because the converter would be easy to change to accept it.

Name new albums after something people will recognize outside
your own machine. A folder name and a display title serve
different readers. The album identifies a place; the title gives
meaning to a picture. Changing a title should not force you
to rename a file, and changing an album should not silently
break a reference somebody else uses. That is a reason to
keep the catalog as data and to tell other people when a
layout changes.

\begin{terminal}
file ./intake/canal.jpg './intake/front door.jpg'
ls -ld ./intake ./stage
\end{terminal}

Expect a description of text, with wording dependent on the tool
and its database. \cmd|file| uses tests on the file rather than
just its suffix; it is useful evidence but not proof an image is
harmless to open. \cmd|ls -ld| shows the directory entries
themselves, including permissions, rather than enumerating
each directory's children. On real files, also consider metadata
privacy: staging a byte-for-byte copy preserves any location
information embedded in the file. This workflow does not remove
metadata or re-encode photos. Chapter~\ref{ch:42} discusses
inspection and non-destructive conversion.

For real work, look at file types and metadata before making
decisions about publication. A modified date may say when the
file was copied, not when a photograph was taken. A device's
clock or timezone may have been wrong even if the photograph
does contain a capture date. A person can supply a useful
description when the machine can't infer one. If the destination
will be public, agree with the contributors on what can be
shared. Preserve the originals privately according to that
agreement. A published copy that deliberately removes location
metadata will no longer be identical in bytes. Keep that
separate from the staging proof here; verification then needs
to check the intended conversion as well as the visible result.
\index{photos!staging}\index{file@\texttt{file}}

\section{Read punctuation as part of the title}

The catalog header names three fields: \texttt{filename} is an
intake basename (the name with no directory prefix), \texttt{album}
chooses a folder under stage, and \texttt{title} is the display
text. This fixture chooses only two albums. Fill a fresh file
with the catalog below. A comma in a title is enclosed by double
quotes in CSV. The outer single quotes in the terminal example
hold each line together as one shell argument and are not part of
the CSV. This is not the same use of quotation marks.

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'filename,album,title' \
    'canal.jpg,water,"Canal, after rain"' \
    'front door.jpg,street,Green front door' > ./catalog.csv
cat ./catalog.csv
\end{terminal}

Splitting every line on commas would yield four pieces on the
canal row. A CSV parser knows that the comma in the quoted field
belongs to the title. A spreadsheet may show a correct column
view, but if you export it again, verify delimiters, encoding,
and column names. Our converter expects UTF-8, comma separators,
and the header shown in the fixture. Other exports may use
semicolons or a different encoding. Reject a mismatched input
rather than turning it quietly into a plausible catalog.
See Chapter~\ref{ch:24} for more about CSV and JSON.
\index{CSV!quoted fields}\index{JSON!photo catalog}

\input{figures/chapter-47/fields.tikz}

Figure~\ref{fig:47-fields} shows the canal record as three
fields. Empty and whitespace-only titles are not useful in
this exhibit; the converter refuses them. The filename rule
allows simple names like \texttt{front door.jpg} but not a
slash, a leading hyphen, a hidden name, or an arbitrary camera
filename. It is a deliberately narrow practice rule, not a
definition of a valid photo filename. Do not use it as a reason
to delete rejected originals. For a real batch, handle case
collisions, leading dots, Unicode normalization and re-used
names before copying across filesystems. Two distinct names on
one filesystem might refer to one name on the destination.

This file format also has an audience. The CSV is a sensible
place to edit small rows with a compatible editor or spreadsheet;
the JSON is an export for systems that expect an array of
records. Choose one authoritative source for changes. If
one person corrects the title in CSV while another corrects
the generated JSON, the next export can quietly discard one of
those fixes. Write down which source to edit, and include
the rules for producing the export with the catalog. If a
program says a catalog failed validation after you saved it
from a spreadsheet, inspect the header and one rejected row
in a plain-text reader before changing the converter. A report
about an unknown album is more useful than silently inventing
a new path from the data.

\begin{quicktable}{p{.29\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
Input & Acceptance rule for this converter \\
\midrule
Header & Exactly three named columns in the shown order. \\
Filename & A simple lower-case \texttt{.jpg} suffix with a
restricted ASCII basename; no duplicate names even after
case folding. \\
Album & Either \texttt{water} or \texttt{street}; no arbitrary
directory path is accepted. \\
Title & Nonblank text, represented as a JSON string. CSV
quoting determines what belongs inside this field. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Preview a plan without carrying it out}\label{sec:47-plan}

Save the supplied \FilePath{listings/chapter-47/catalog.py} as
\texttt{catalog.py} in the fixture root or transcribe the listing
in an editor. No knowledge of Python syntax is needed to use it.
The converter reads the named CSV and prints JSON only after
validating all records. It writes no photo and runs no command
from the CSV. The program's Python source is a script, not
terminal input. Run it using the explicit \cmd|python3|
interpreter. POSIX shell syntax appears in the terminal examples;
the converter itself is optional and uses only Python's standard
library.

\lstinputlisting[style=utpcode]{listings/chapter-47/catalog.py}

\begin{terminal}
python3 ./catalog.py ./catalog.csv
\end{terminal}

Expect a JSON array with an object for each row. The canal object
has title \texttt{Canal, after rain}. Its \texttt{source} is
\texttt{intake/canal.jpg}, and its \texttt{destination} is
\texttt{stage/water/canal.jpg}. The second source has a space.
JSON quotes delimit strings; they are not shell escaping.
Do not paste values as shell syntax, feed them to \cmd|eval|,
or interpret the printed JSON as a list of commands. A plan
can have syntactically valid JSON and still send photos to the
wrong albums. Compare both entries with the CSV and the directory
names before proceeding.

The output names use slash separators because the plan
describes this Unix fixture. The checker validates each name as
a separate field, then constructs a path under the known
prefix. It does not inspect the files, check access, or acquire
permission to write. That is deliberate: generating a plan
should be safe when a destination does not exist yet. It also
means even a valid plan cannot tell you whether a path already
points somewhere unexpected. Make the staging directory fresh,
private, and under your control. On a shared system, do not
run a copying routine on paths that another person can swap
while you are reviewing them.

A record contains both the original and the destination,
rather than only the new name. That makes two reviews
possible. You can read the CSV and confirm the new location
for each original; someone else can start at the finished
location and look up which original it should match. Review
one record from each direction before you copy. In a project
of hundreds of photos, comparing the total number of plan
rows and copies is a useful coarse check. It is not sufficient:
one extra copy and one omitted copy can cancel in the count.
Account for each intended pair.

After a successful preview, generate the saved catalog. The
\cmd|&&| runs the second command only if the first succeeds.
If validation reports an error, the redirection can still leave
an empty file. A saved catalog is ready only after the converter
has succeeded and you have reviewed its contents.

\begin{terminal}
python3 ./catalog.py ./catalog.csv > ./catalog.json &&
    cat ./catalog.json
\end{terminal}

In this practice, make the two album directories inside the
new \texttt{stage} directory, then copy only the files you
reviewed. Stop if directory creation fails. If either destination
already exists, stop rather than agreeing to an overwrite;
\cmd|cp -i| asks before overwriting a file in this interactive
exercise. An existing wrong target can also be a directory,
which is why starting with an empty private staging area
matters. Do not interpret \cmd|-i| as an atomic no-clobber
guarantee in a directory someone else can change.

\begin{terminal}
mkdir ./stage/water ./stage/street
cp -i ./intake/canal.jpg ./stage/water/canal.jpg
cp -i './intake/front door.jpg' './stage/street/front door.jpg'
\end{terminal}

\input{figures/chapter-47/verify.tikz}

\section{Verify content and placement separately}

\cmd|cmp| compares bytes. Ordinarily it reports the first
difference, if any. Silence with zero status means two readable
files compare equal byte for byte. A nonzero status means a
difference or trouble; investigate rather than replacing the
copy automatically. Check the second copy too. If you were
verifying an actual collection, the important question would
not be ``did any comparison succeed?'' but ``was there a
comparison for every planned copy?'' Figure~\ref{fig:47-verify}
shows one such pair.

If one pair differs, don't immediately copy again. Ask which
source version the plan meant, whether the destination came
from this attempt or a previous one, and whether there was
an intentional conversion. Look at the actual operands of
the failing comparison. Comparing a green door with a blue
canal is a mistake you can reproduce safely in the exercise;
matching both filenames at a glance is no substitute for
checking the two paths. In a real failure, preserve both
copies while you decide what went wrong. If corrections
are needed, use a new versioned staging directory so you can
compare the two attempts without destroying the evidence.

\begin{terminal}
cmp ./intake/canal.jpg ./stage/water/canal.jpg
printf 'canal comparison: %s\n' "$?"
cmp './intake/front door.jpg' './stage/street/front door.jpg'
printf 'door comparison: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|cksum| without algorithm options computes a POSIX checksum
and reports a byte count. Give it standard input with \cmd|<|
and the output will not depend on the different path spelling.
The two numbers for the canal original and copy should match.
This is a portable accidental-change check, not a secure
cryptographic digest and not proof of authorship. If a project
specifies SHA-256, choose the documented utility and record
the algorithm and source of the expected digest. Utility names
and options vary across GNU and BSD systems (Chapter~\ref{ch:23}).
\index{cmp@\texttt{cmp}}\index{cksum@\texttt{cksum}}

\begin{terminal}
cksum < ./intake/canal.jpg
cksum < ./stage/water/canal.jpg
find ./stage -type f -print
\end{terminal}

\cmd|find| walks the stage tree, \cmd|-type f| selects regular
files, and \cmd|-print| prints each selected path. Expect
exactly the two staged files; their order is not important.
Our controlled names do not contain newlines. For arbitrary
files, newline-delimited output is for inspection, not reliable
input to a script that guesses where each name ends.

\begin{quicktable}{p{.30\linewidth}Y}
\toprule
Check & What it cannot establish \\
\midrule
CSV parsed & The files exist, their types are as expected, or
the titles are the right ones. \\
Staging tree listed & The named copies have the right bytes. \\
Byte comparison passed & Metadata is safe to publish or the
chosen album is correct. \\
Checksums match & The originals are safely backed up or the
files are authenticated. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Identical but wrong}
\Brian{The two copies match their originals. Is the exhibit ready?}
\Stephen{The copy step worked. I was about to call that the whole job.}
\Brian{Then why does the canal appear under street in my catalog?}
\Stephen{Because the byte comparison checked a pair of files, not the exhibit labels.}
\Brian{And a byte-for-byte copy has any location data the original had.}
\Stephen{Yes. Review the plan and the metadata separately from the copies.}
\Brian{Then keeping the originals gives me somewhere to start again.}
\DialogueConclusion{Verify the copy and the plan separately.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Leave enough information to redo the staging}

If another volunteer will take over next week, your handoff
should let them audit the arrangement without needing your
terminal history. Name the particular intake collection and
the catalog version, list the accepted album names, and say
whether the staging operation copied or converted the files.
Record any exclusions and what should happen to a row that
failed to validate. ``The unrecognized album is waiting for
a decision'' is better than letting that picture vanish
from the conversation. Don't put private source paths,
credentials, or sensitive metadata into a shared handoff.

Try to answer a concrete question from the saved artifacts:
``Which intake file should the canal copy in the water album
match?'' The JSON gives the two path fragments, the directory
listing locates the copy, and the comparison establishes that
the bytes matched when you tested them. If an editor changes
the title after this check, update the authoritative CSV
and export again, but do not infer that a new file must be
copied. If the editor changes the chosen album, the staged
path is now part of what needs checking. Separating these
questions avoids a full recopy after a spelling correction.

Finally, decide how to handle the handoff itself. The stage
directory is convenient for review but isn't a verified
backup just because it contains copies. Losing its disk can
lose intake and stage together. Keep the project backup on
storage appropriate to the group, and test that you can recover
the authoritative catalog along with the photos. Label a
temporary stage so a teammate doesn't think it is the last
remaining original. The next chapter makes the retrieval and
restore side of that check explicit.

\begin{takeaways}
\item Make a reviewed staging plan before copying or renaming.
\item Parse structured data with tools that understand the format.
\item Verify both paths and bytes, and keep publication a separate step.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Keep the comma inside its field}\label{ex:47-comma}
With the existing CSV and JSON, find the canal title in each.
Explain the two roles of the quotes without changing either file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
grep -n -F -e 'Canal, after rain' ./catalog.csv ./catalog.json
\end{terminal}
The phrase occupies one quoted CSV field and one JSON string.
Use the printed catalog instead if you previewed it without
saving it. No extra file is needed.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Reject the unknown album}\label{ex:47-album}
Write \texttt{trial.csv} with the same header and a row for
\texttt{canal.jpg} in album \texttt{parking}. Run the converter
without redirection and check its status. Remove the trial
file during cleanup. If Python 3 is not available, describe
which condition rejects it and continue to the third exercise.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'filename,album,title' \
    'canal.jpg,parking,Canal' > ./trial.csv
python3 ./catalog.py ./trial.csv
printf 'trial status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
Expect a diagnostic for an unknown album, no JSON, and status
one. Neither existing catalog is replaced.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Spot a mismatched pair}\label{ex:47-pair}
Compare the canal intake file against the staged door file by
mistake on purpose. Check the status and then compare the
correct canal pair. No modification is needed.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
cmp ./intake/canal.jpg './stage/street/front door.jpg'
printf 'mismatched pair: %s\n' "$?"
cmp ./intake/canal.jpg ./stage/water/canal.jpg
printf 'correct pair: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
The first comparison reports a difference and status one,
the second is silent with status zero.
\end{solution}

Run \cmd|pwd| and verify that the entire path matches what
you recorded during setup. Stop if it differs. Remove only
the practice paths below; \cmd|rm -i| asks before removing
each file, so check the name before answering. Omit the script and generated catalog if
you did not use Python, and \texttt{trial.csv} if unused.
The originals in a real photo project would not be removed
as part of staging cleanup.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i './stage/street/front door.jpg' ./stage/water/canal.jpg
rm -i './intake/front door.jpg' ./intake/canal.jpg
rm -i ./catalog.csv ./catalog.json ./catalog.py ./trial.csv
rmdir ./stage/water ./stage/street ./stage ./intake
cd / && rmdir "$lab"
\end{terminal}

